= Definitions

Denote $Lambda$ the set of all lambda terms and $arr_beta$ the beta reduction. We define a rewrite system as a couple $(V,E)$ of verticices and $E subset.eq V^2$ the rewrite rules. It's basically an ordered graph. We denote by $C chevron x chevron.r $ the hole context $C$. We therefore have that $arr_beta$ is the set of the form $ C chevron.l (lambda x. e ) e' chevron.r arr_beta C chevron.l e[x <- e'] chevron.r $

Let $Lambda_I$ be the set of identity-terms generated by the grammar $e, e' ::= (lambda x. x) | e space e'$. It is a subset of all the lambda terms. One can see that $Lambda_I$ is closed under $arr_beta$, with $arr_beta$ being in this case of the form 
$ C chevron.l I e chevron.r arr_beta C chevron.l e chevron.r $

Let $cal(L)_I$ be the set of words on $Sigma = {A,I}$ generated by the grammar $cal(G) : w, w' ::= I | A w w'$. We define a re-write system $(cal(L)_I,arr_AI)$ with the re-write rule "we can remove a occurence of the factor $AI$", formally :
$ w A I w' arr_AI w w' $
*Remark* The set $cal(L)_I$ is very close to the set of Dyck words. We indeed have the following characterisation
$ cal(L)_I = { w in Sigma^* | |w|_A = |w|_I -1 "and" forall w' "prefix strict of" w, |w'|_A >= |w'|_I }$


This gives us that :
- All words are of odd length
- The only normal form is $I$
- All words of length $> 1$ start with an $A$
- The grammar of $cal(L)_I$ is not ambiguous (the classical proof for the Dyck's words work, just with a -1)

*Proof of the characterisation* Denote $D^o$ the characterisation set. By induction, it is clear that $cal(L)_I subset.eq D^o$. Let's prove the other inclusion by induction on the length of the word. Let $w := w_1...w_n in D^o$. If $w_1 = I$, then $w = I in cal(L)_I$. Otherwise, $w_1 = A$ and let $i$ be the first natural such than $|w_2 ... w_i|_A = |w_2 ... w_i|_I -1$. Denote $u = w_2 ... w_i$ and $v = w_(i+1) ... w_n$. Then one can quickly check that $u,v in D^o$ and we have $w = A u v in cal(L)_I$ as $u,v in cal(L)_I$ by induction.

*Proof of the non-ambigueity of $cal(G)$*
Take a word $w$ of minimal length that can be written in two different way. Then $w != I$, so $w = A u_1 u_2 = A v_1 v_2$. By minimality of the length, $|u_1| != |v_1|$, so suppose without loss of generality $|u_1| < |v_1|$ (the other way is symmetric). Then $u_1$ is a prefix of $v_1$ with $|u_1|_A = |u_1|_I - 1$, a contradiction.


